  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nSort of a tale of two days situation across the area so far today \nwith a couple of waves of showers/weak thunderstorm activity. The \nfirst of which has already all but moved out of the area, and the \nsecond more recently moving into western areas of the CWA. In \nbetween, areas roughly east of US-65 are even enjoying some sunshine \npeeking through. All of this activity is broadly associated with a \nmid-level shortwave trough lifting NE through the region and smaller \nembedded disturbances and will continue to linger periodic scattered \nshower and weak thunderstorm activity into Sunday morning before \nmore substantial drying and mid-level height rises move in. While \ninitial hi-res guidance suggested relative drying or lesser activity \ninto this afternoon, showers and weak thunderstorm activity may end \nup being more prevalent than initially thought across portions of \nfar eastern Kansas and into western/northwest Missouri. This due to \nMCV-like depictions noted on radar throughout the day as activity \nhas lifted into/through Kansas. Coupled with the broader lift with \nthe mid-level wave, tend to suspect an uptick in weak \nshower/thundershower activity and have begun to be seen on radar as \nof 2pm in NW Missouri. Nothing of significance aside from a rumble \nof thunder possible and brief heavy rain. And then overnight, LLJ \nnoses into the area and should again blossom at least scattered \nshower/thundershower activity into the area. Currently, chances \novernight tend to be best south of Highway 36. Previously mentioned \nmid-level height rises slide in Sunday, drying conditions out \nthrough the day. \n\nAn interesting, likely quite impactful, pattern sets up across CONUS \nduring the work week. This being current nor'easter milling about, a \nwestern CONUS trough/cutoff low moving in-land, the remnants of \nPacific hurricanes Polo and Odalys lifting northward across Baja \nCalifornia into the increasingly SW flow pattern, and Gulf moisture \nstreaming northward through the Plains. At this point in time, there \nis high confidence in the broad scale pattern evolution through at \nleast mid-week, if not the entire work-week. And in effect this \nspells out a heavy rain/flooding threat that runs from the \ncentral/southern Intermountain West and into the Southern to Central \nPlains. For the immediate area, this potential tends to reside at \nDays 4/5 (Tuesday/Wednesday) and beyond. For added confidence in the \npotential, GFS/Euro ensembles (as well as their AI versions) \nhighlight and expansive/broad area of 2-4+ inch QPF through mid-late \nweek, which is impressive when you consider the washed out nature of \nensemble QPF. The potential is further highlighted by deterministic \nsolutions, including the 12z GFS, depicting PWats climbing toward \nand above 2 inches (highly anomalous this time of the year) and \nswaths/pockets 6-8+ inches QPF. But given the time horizon, there is \na good degree of uncertainty in the finer details, such as the exact \ntrack of the mid-level cut-off low, its associated surface low, and \nfrontal positions so will stress that locations of peak amounts \nremain uncertain. After an expansive (geographically) coordination \ncall with WPC today, broad Slight Risks exist on Days 4/5 for the \nWPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook product, with likely upgrades as \nfiner details come into better focus. Lastly, the current best \npotential tends to reside over the NW half of the CWA or so, \nespecially as you consider those areas have the most saturated soils \nas well (via NASA SPoRT).",
